# tests/recursion/define.rxt -- module `recursion` ([DD-14]): D71 item 4: `(?(DEFINE)...)` is module `recursion`'s, tailed off the `(?(` doorway and lowered as the `{0}`-callee shape
#
# GENERATED BY tests/recursion/gen_corpus.py, and that is a property rather
# than a convenience: every expectation below -- including every `g` line --
# was produced by driving the cell through libpcre2 10.46 (via the committed
# ctypes binding at docs/design/subroutines_measurements/probes/sr_oracle.py,
# which borrows la_oracle.py -> br_oracle.py -> pcre2_ctypes.py) BEFORE it was
# written. The generator never asks pcrec anything.
#
# THE SOLE ORACLE IS LIBPCRE2 -- THERE IS NO PYTHON ARM, AND THAT IS AN
# ABSENCE RATHER THAN A DIVERGENCE (design SS10.1). python3 `re` has no
# subroutine-call construct at all: every one of the nine call spellings plus
# both zero spellings raises `re.error` at compile time. `\\1` and `(?P=n)`
# DO compile in python, but they are `backrefs`' reference construct, not this
# module's call construct (design SS2.1's one-cell discriminator is why
# `spellings.rxt` opens with it) -- so marking this corpus python-verifiable
# on the strength of those two spellings would be checking the wrong module.
#
# `(?(DEFINE)...)` IS THIS MODULE'S SINCE [DD-14] WAVE F (D71 item 4,
# overruling design SS2.5's "RULED: no DEFINE"). It is a TAILED row on the
# `(?(` doorway -- the rest of `(?(` is still `conditionals`' -- and it lowers
# to the SAME NODE the `{0}`-callee idiom produces, an A_REP with
# rmin == rmax == 0 over the body. That is why define.rxt and zerodef.rxt
# carry the two spellings SIDE BY SIDE on the same cells: they are two
# spellings of one construct, and the corpus asserts they agree rather than
# assuming it. Where a body must be callee-only, `{0}` remains the spelling
# used in the OTHER files, because those cells were oracle-verified in that
# form and re-spelling them would move a measurement without re-measuring it.
#
# NOTHING IN src/ IMPLEMENTS A SUBROUTINE CALL YET. Every ordinary `m`/`n`
# block in this corpus is therefore EXPECTED to report a pattern-compile
# failure under today's harness (`requires module 'recursion'`) -- that is
# the correct, documented state before [DD-14] wave B+C lands (docs/
# testing.md's "expected-unsupported" policy), not a corpus defect.
#
# Design: docs/design/subroutines_design.md. Decisions: docs/dev/decisions.md
# D71 (the give-up-code axis, D71.1) and D72 (PCREC_ERR_INTERNAL).

# ====================================================================
# THE LIBRARY IDIOM ITSELF (D71 item 4). `(?(DEFINE)BODY)` is a conditional group whose condition is never true, so BODY never runs where it is written and exists only to be CALLED. That is exactly what `(?:BODY){0}` means, and wave F lowers the two to the SAME NODE -- see zerodef.rxt, where every cell carries both spellings side by side.
# ====================================================================

# the shape a user copies out of a library: define once, call twice. The DEFINE body leaves group 1 UNSET, which the `g` line records -- the definition never ran.
pattern (?(DEFINE)(?<w>[a-z]+))^(?&w)-(?&w)$
features recursion,named-groups
m "foo-bar" 0 7
g 1 -1 -1
n "-bar"
n "foo-"

# a NUMBERED callee inside the DEFINE: the groups in the body number normally and are callable by number, so this needs no `named-groups` at all.
pattern (?(DEFINE)([a-z]+))^(?1)-(?1)$
features recursion
m "foo-bar" 0 7
g 1 -1 -1

# THE BODY DOES NOT RUN LEXICALLY, and "xa" is the cell that says so: if the DEFINE consumed its body this would match and "a" would not.
pattern ^(?(DEFINE)(?<w>x))a$
features recursion,named-groups
m "a" 0 1
g 1 -1 -1
n "xa"

# TWO definitions in ONE DEFINE -- a concatenation is one branch, which is what PCRE2's single-branch rule permits.
pattern (?(DEFINE)(?<a>x)(?<b>y))^(?&a)(?&b)$
features recursion,named-groups
m "xy" 0 2
g 1 -1 -1
g 2 -1 -1

# A RECURSIVE CALLEE DEFINED IN A DEFINE. The language is a^n b^n, which is not regular -- the whole reason the CALL rows are VM_ONLY, reached through the definition doorway.
pattern (?(DEFINE)(?<p>a(?&p)?b))^(?&p)$
features recursion,named-groups
m "ab" 0 2
g 1 -1 -1
m "aabb" 0 4
g 1 -1 -1
n "aab"

# THE DEFINE COMES AFTER THE CALL. A forward call is legal by construction (design SS2.3) because the resolver runs at end of parse, and a DEFINE is just a place a group is declared.
pattern ^(?&w)$(?(DEFINE)(?<w>[a-z]+))
features recursion,named-groups
m "foo" 0 3
g 1 -1 -1

# AN ALTERNATION *INSIDE* the defined group is fine -- PCRE2's single-branch rule is about the DEFINE's own top level, not about what a group in the body may contain. The refusal cell below is the other half of this pair.
pattern (?(DEFINE)(?<w>a|b))^(?&w)$
features recursion,named-groups
m "a" 0 1
g 1 -1 -1
m "b" 0 1
g 1 -1 -1

# AN EMPTY DEFINE COMPILES, measured on 10.46. The brief for this wave expected a refusal for a body that defines nothing; libpcre2 has no such rule, so neither does pcrec.
pattern (?(DEFINE))^x$
features recursion
m "x" 0 1

# AND SO DOES A BODY THAT DEFINES NO GROUP AT ALL. Also measured, also not a refusal.
pattern (?(DEFINE)abc)^x$
features recursion
m "x" 0 1

# A QUANTIFIED DEFINE. A zero-width construct is still a repeatable item -- the row reads `quantifiable yes` because of this cell, not because of a reading of the docs.
pattern (?(DEFINE)(?<w>a))*^x$
features recursion,named-groups
m "x" 0 1
g 1 -1 -1

# THE SCOPED OPTION STATE reaches the body: `(?i)` set before the DEFINE applies inside it, and the port saves and restores `cx->mods` around the body parse exactly as every other body-carrying port does.
pattern (?i)(?(DEFINE)(?<w>a))^(?&w)$
features recursion,named-groups,modifiers
m "A" 0 1
g 1 -1 -1

# TWO DEFINEs in one pattern.
pattern (?(DEFINE)(?<w>a))(?(DEFINE)(?<v>b))^(?&w)(?&v)$
features recursion,named-groups
m "ab" 0 2
g 1 -1 -1
g 2 -1 -1

# ====================================================================
# THE ONE REFUSAL, AND IT IS PCRE2'S OWN RULE rather than a pcrec limit: a DEFINE's own top level may carry only ONE branch. pcrec reproduces PCRE2's wording at PCRE2's own offset -- MEASURED at `at + 3` (the byte after `(?(`, where the condition begins) in three positions.
# ====================================================================

# two branches, each defining a group.
# libpcre2 ALSO refuses to compile this: pcre2_compile failed at offset 3: DEFINE subpattern contains more than one branch
pattern (?(DEFINE)(?<w>a)|(?<v>b))^(?&w)$
features recursion,named-groups
perr

# two branches defining nothing -- the rule is about the BRANCH COUNT and not about what the branches contain.
# libpcre2 ALSO refuses to compile this: pcre2_compile failed at offset 3: DEFINE subpattern contains more than one branch
pattern (?(DEFINE)a|b)^x$
features recursion
perr

# ====================================================================
# THE TAIL IS `DEFINE)` AND IT INCLUDES THE PARENTHESIS, which is what keeps this row from claiming conditions that are `conditionals`'. MEASURED: on 10.46 a lowercase or truncated keyword is read as a NAME condition, so both of these are 'reference to non-existent subpattern' there -- and here they are the `conditionals` doorway's refusal, with `recursion` ENABLED in both cells.
# ====================================================================

# lowercase `define` is NOT the keyword.
# libpcre2 ALSO refuses to compile this: pcre2_compile failed at offset 3: reference to non-existent subpattern
pattern (?(define)(?<w>a))^x$
features recursion,named-groups
perr

# a PREFIX of the keyword is not the keyword either -- and without the `)` in the tail, `(?(DEFINED)` would have been claimed by this module too.
# libpcre2 ALSO refuses to compile this: pcre2_compile failed at offset 3: reference to non-existent subpattern
pattern (?(DEF)(?<w>a))^x$
features recursion,named-groups
perr
